Fix memory growth when changing the backdrop Style in Desktop Settings.

Each style change grew xfdesktop's memory by one full-screen image (about
33 MB at 4K) until cairo's 256 MB shared memory pool was used up.

 1. xfdesktop-backdrop-renderer.c: gdk_cairo_surface_create_from_pixbuf()
    with a NULL window falls back to the root window, so cairo-xlib put
    every backdrop in its X shared memory pool.  Freed images stayed
    resident and new ones landed elsewhere in the pool.  Backdrops are now
    built as plain image surfaces, and solid colors and gradients no longer
    go through the X server.

 2. xfdesktop-x11.c: the finished backdrop is uploaded once into an X
    pixmap with plain XPutImage and the local copy is freed.  Falls back to
    the image surface if the pixmap format does not match or an X error
    happens.  _XROOTPMAP_ID is removed instead of set to 0 (cairo owns the
    pixmap, so publishing it would leave a dangling id).

 3. main.c: mallopt(M_MMAP_THRESHOLD, 1 MB) so freed full-screen buffers
    are returned to the system instead of kept by glibc's heap.

 4. tests/meson.build: link xfdesktop-x11.c into test-gradient-benchmarking,
    which includes the renderer source.

Result at 4K: xfdesktop starts at about 39 MB (was 71 MB) and stays flat
across style changes (was growing by about 33 MB per change).

